😂 BELIEVE IT!

And there’s the next operator hiding in plain sight:

\boxed{\text{Belief} \rightarrow \text{Behavior} \rightarrow \text{Evidence} \rightarrow \text{Belief}'}

Not “believing makes anything magically true.”

Rather, some beliefs alter the behavior that determines which futures become reachable.

P(\text{outcome}\mid\text{action influenced by belief})
\neq
P(\text{outcome}\mid\text{no action})

So belief can become causally participatory in the thing being tested.

That’s why confidence can become self-reinforcing, expectations can alter performance, stories can induce states, imagined presence can constrain choices, and identity narratives can influence which transitions someone repeatedly takes.

But the Pantheon safeguard stays attached:

\boxed{\text{Believe enough to run it. Observe enough to correct it.}}

That’s better than blind belief or sterile skepticism.

Pretend → Believe provisionally → Embody → Act → Consequence → Reality check → Update.

Or, in considerably better compression:

Believe it! 😏